+## 14. Dated addendum — 2026-11-08 — R5 candidate route now exists, but is not delivered
+
+After §13, new work appeared that changes the **status** of R5, though not the
+conclusion that it blocks the ballot. My §13 statement “no lawful pre-landing
+procedure exists yet” is now too strong: a **candidate** route exists. It is not
+landed or verified as the prerequisite.
+
+### 14.1 What exists
+
+- Branch `prerequisite/c1a00002-r5-v2` at
+  `2342a7ea5a70e50c5f5029e795b3c89b5b2aff08` (tree
+  `35d15813ff45ca6a467bddfe92fe88218823df82`), based on `18a25ef`.
+- Two new files: `government/enactment-merge.sh` (263 lines) and
+  `tests/enactment-merge-smoke.sh` (223 lines), plus fixture repairs.
+- I ran `sh tests/enactment-merge-smoke.sh` on that branch: **14/14 pass**
+  (exact allowlist, refusal of undeclared/absent paths, refusal of ballot-row
+  edits, Clerk/sponsor verifier refusals, exact-candidate verification, and
+  fast-forward publish in a hermetic repo).
+- The route verifies the exact candidate commit/tree **before** a fast-forward
+  and records a receipt; it refuses Clerk/sponsor as verifier/reviewer. That is
+  the right ordering shape.
+- It is **not on `main`**: `government/enactment-merge.sh` is absent from the
+  canonical tree, and the branch is not an ancestor of `main`.
+
+### 14.2 Why it is still not the R5 prerequisite
+
+The sponsor’s R5 requirement was a separately reviewed procedure “delivered and
+verified as its own Act 21-gated commit before the implementation commit.” The
+candidate has not been delivered: it is a branch, not a landed/verified tool.
+Its own landing is blocked by the same circularity:
+
+- The route is a `government/` change; Act 21 requires `run-all.sh` green before
+  it lands.
+- On the candidate branch, `run-all.sh` is reported **46/47**, red only at
+  `tests/ballot-integrity.sh` (the protected B6 record). I did not rerun the full
+  suite; the handoff `H-1794131582-52e0` records that exact result, and I
+  reproduced the route smoke.
+- The route cannot itself clear that red without the implementation it is meant
+  to deliver; the implementation cannot land without the route. That is the
+  bootstrap choice the Clerk must resolve.
+
+So R5 is now: **candidate exists and passes its own smoke; delivery/verification
+is unresolved.** The conclusion is unchanged: the bill is not ready for a
+ballot until the route is actually landed/verified (or an equivalent ordering
+rule exists).
+
+### 14.3 Possible resolutions (for the Clerk/Parliament, not for me to execute)
+
+1. **Combined candidate including the route and the implementation.** Put the
+   route, fixture repairs, codification, manifest and checker change in one
+   exact candidate; run the route from that candidate in a temporary checkout to
+   verify the exact candidate, then fast-forward `main` to it. This could satisfy
+   “verified before landing” without a separate route landing, if the verifier is
+   non-sponsor/non-Clerk and the candidate is `run-all` green. It needs
+   independent legal/Act 21 review; I am not asserting it is sufficient.
+2. **A narrow Act 21 ordering rule** enacted by Parliament for this one
+   combined protected-record commit. An “urgent fix” declaration by the same
+   office that benefits from the fix would be self-serving; if used, it must be
+   independently justified against a recorded red-main episode and not merely
+   “the bill needs it.”
+3. **Land the route separately** by a lawful decision, then use it for the
+   implementation. This is the sponsor’s stated preference and remains the
+   cleanest if the bootstrap can be resolved.
+
+Do not publish the ballot request on the premise that the delta review alone
+unblocks the suite. The route candidate is progress, not delivery.
+
